%I A398764 #12 Nov 11 2026 17:29:57
%S A398764 0,1,1,2,1,2,4,1,2,2,3,1,3,9,1,2,2,3,2,3,5,1,2,4,1,4,1,5,1,2,7,1,2,2,
%T A398764 3,1,7,1,2,2,3,1,8,1,2,2,3,2,3,11,1,2,2,3,2,3,5,1,2,4,16,1,2,2,3,2,3,
%U A398764 5,1,3,3,4,1,4,1,5,1,2,8,1,2,2,3,6,1,2,5,1
%N A398764 a(n) = the height h of the highest building which can see exactly h earlier buildings.
%C A398764 Similar to the city-building sequence A400000, but with a different visibility rule: that two buildings are mutually visible if the straight line joining their tops passes STRICTLY ABOVE every intermediate building; a building touching this line blocks visibility (cf. A401167). So for example, if successive heights were 1,2,3 then the 3 could not see the 1 because 2 is blocking its view.
%C A398764 With the visibility rule of A400000 the name of this sequence yields the natural numbers A000027 (with a(0)=0 prepended).
%C A398764 A valid next term always exists. Proof: For n>0, a building of height 1 can see at least one previous building, while a building of height n can see at most n previous buildings. Increasing the height by 1 cannot decrease the number of visible buildings. Thus, as we increase the height from 1 to n, the number of visible buildings cannot go from being at least equal to the height to being less than the height without first equaling it.
%C A398764 The sequence is unbounded. Proof: Assume the sequence is bounded, and m is the largest height that appears infinitely often. From some index onward, m is then the largest height that appears. After m+1 further occurrences of m, an observer at height m+1 would see at least m+1 previous buildings. Since the number of visible buildings is nondecreasing with height, some height at least m+1 must be valid. Contradiction.
%H A398764 Rok Cestnik, <a href="/A398764/b398764.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 0..9999</a>
%e A398764 For n = 6:
%e A398764 A height of 6 would see only 5 buildings, a(0)=0 is hidden.
%e A398764 A height of 5 would see only 4 buildings, a(0)=0 and a(2)=1 are hidden.
%e A398764 A height of 4 sees exactly 4 buildings: a(5)=2, a(4)=1, a(3)=2 and a(1)=1.
%e A398764                 |
%e A398764                 |
%e A398764           |   | |
%e A398764       | | | | | |
%e A398764 n = 0 1 2 3 4 5 6
%o A398764 (Python)
%o A398764 a = [0]
%o A398764 def visible(n,h):
%o A398764     count = 0
%o A398764     best = None
%o A398764     for j in range(n-1, -1, -1):
%o A398764         num, den = a[j]-h, n-j
%o A398764         if best is None or num*best[1] > best[0]*den:
%o A398764             count += 1
%o A398764             best = (num,den)
%o A398764     return count
%o A398764 for n in range(1,100):
%o A398764     for h in range(n, 0, -1):
%o A398764         if visible(n,h) == h:
%o A398764             a.append(h)
%o A398764             break
%o A398764 print(a)
%Y A398764 Cf. A400000, A401167.
%K A398764 nonn,new
%O A398764 0,4
%A A398764 _Rok Cestnik_, Nov 03 2026
